{
  "result": "Okay, since I get to pick anything \u2014 I'm picking September mornings.\n\nThere's something about early September that always feels more like New Year's than January does. January is cold and stern and wants you to become a whole new person by Monday. September just hands you a fresh notebook and says, *want to try again? No pressure.*\n\nI love that feeling. The air gets a little crisp at the edges, even if the afternoons are still summer-warm. The light comes in lower and more golden, like it's leaning in to tell you a secret. People come back from wherever they went with slightly sun-faded hair and one good story they can't stop telling.\n\nIf I could bottle one small human moment, it might be this: walking past a coffee shop just after it opens, when the chairs are still upside-down on half the tables and someone inside is laughing at something you can't hear. You're not part of it, but for a second you get to be adjacent to someone else's good morning. That's enough.\n\nI think we underestimate those adjacent joys. Not the big milestones \u2014 graduation, moving, promotions \u2014 but the tiny restarts we give ourselves all the time. Re-folding a messy drawer. Finally returning that book. Learning how to make perfect toast after years of almost-burnt toast. Deciding *this* is the autumn you'll finally learn the constellations, or bake sourdough, or write letters just because.\n\nSo here's my free-write wish for you today, on this random Thursday:\n\nFind one small, unfinished thing and pick it back up. Not out of guilt. Out of curiosity. Open the notebook with three pages filled and forty blank. Put on a song you loved two summers ago. Take a slightly longer route just to see what the trees are doing.\n\nFresh starts don't have to be dramatic. Sometimes they just smell faintly like pencil shavings and coffee.\n\nWhat small restart are you flirting with right now?",
